1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
Thank you.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
Thank you.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:29,980
Thank you.

4
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:32,360
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here this morning.

5
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:35,620
Super excited that you have joined us this morning.

6
00:01:35,620 --> 00:01:41,060
We are launching a brand new series, which I will talk about in just a minute.

7
00:01:41,880 --> 00:01:45,440
But for now, I want to recap where we've been this far this year.

8
00:01:45,440 --> 00:01:51,560
So we just finished up nine weeks of the spiritual formation, spiritual practices series.

9
00:01:52,200 --> 00:02:04,780
And the whole point of that series, and it's going to continue on and off for a while, is that we be with Jesus in order to become like Jesus so that we can do what he did, right?

10
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:06,700
And so far, we've talked about prayer.

11
00:02:06,700 --> 00:02:07,700
We've talked about fasting.

12
00:02:07,700 --> 00:02:11,660
I've heard many stories from many of you who have tried new different types of prayer.

13
00:02:11,660 --> 00:02:19,580
Maybe you've tried fasting or some version of fasting, or you brought a new rhythm of fasting into your life.

14
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:24,360
And I just want to remind all of us that the whole point of this is to pursue Jesus in relationship.

15
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:28,580
That's what the whole point of this thing is all about.

16
00:02:28,580 --> 00:02:34,780
We are a church of real people with real lives that are trying to form a Christ-centered community.

17
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:41,780
We want to know Jesus by spending time with Jesus in order to become like Jesus so that we can show others what he's like in the world.

18
00:02:41,780 --> 00:02:49,920
So I hope that you don't just incorporate these things in your life for eight weeks and then forget about them and wait until the next series to try the new thing.

19
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:56,600
I hope, I pray, that you would consider to continue these rhythms in your life if they are helping you pursue Jesus.

20
00:02:56,600 --> 00:03:02,960
If they are not, then put them on a shelf somewhere until later and find something else that helps you pursue Jesus.

21
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:03,440
Sound good?

22
00:03:03,440 --> 00:03:04,840
All right.

23
00:03:04,840 --> 00:03:07,260
This week, we turn our attention to the Easter narrative.

24
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:13,140
We are starting a three-week series in preparation for Easter Sunday called A Living Savior.

25
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:19,860
Christians, myself included, we tend to gravitate towards the cross.

26
00:03:20,320 --> 00:03:20,460
Right?

27
00:03:20,460 --> 00:03:22,240
Calvary is enough.

28
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:23,380
It's a beautiful thing.

29
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:24,400
We just sang about it.

30
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:33,520
And it's great that we gravitate towards the cross because Jesus said on the cross, it is finished.

31
00:03:33,520 --> 00:03:38,900
But cross means nothing apart from the resurrection.

32
00:03:39,820 --> 00:03:50,600
So, what does it look like for us as Christians to submit to a risen Savior who accomplishes his victory, his life, the whole thing, the whole point?

33
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:54,780
He accomplished it through death and then resurrection.

34
00:03:54,780 --> 00:03:57,540
And isn't it like that for our lives?

35
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:04,940
Don't often we find resurrection on the other side of sorrow, pain, heartache, loss.

36
00:04:06,140 --> 00:04:12,540
We celebrate, we worship, we submit to a Savior who accomplishes victory through death.

37
00:04:12,540 --> 00:04:16,520
So, the next three weeks, we're going to focus on a living Savior.

38
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,220
Let's pray and then we will get started.

39
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:25,340
Father, we are so grateful again for this opportunity to set everything aside.

40
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:33,500
All of our worries, all of our fears, all of our anxieties, all of our sin.

41
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:36,980
And lay it at your feet.

42
00:04:36,980 --> 00:04:40,180
Set our minds' attention on you this morning.

43
00:04:40,180 --> 00:04:46,780
Holy Spirit, I pray that you would be at work in this room through my words, through the reading of Scripture.

44
00:04:46,780 --> 00:04:50,820
God, just doing supernatural things to draw us to yourself.

45
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:54,500
God, we want to know you more.

46
00:04:56,580 --> 00:05:01,500
So, lead us this time, this morning, for your glory and for our good.

47
00:05:01,500 --> 00:05:02,800
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

48
00:05:02,800 --> 00:05:03,340
Amen.

49
00:05:03,340 --> 00:05:07,660
If you have your Bibles, I'd love for you to turn to Mark chapter 14.

50
00:05:07,660 --> 00:05:11,760
Mark chapter 14 is going to be our text for this morning.

51
00:05:11,760 --> 00:05:17,600
And Mercer's out of town, and somehow I made it to church without the Bible that I like to preach with.

52
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:19,720
So, that happened.

53
00:05:19,860 --> 00:05:21,440
So, I found this in Lost and Found.

54
00:05:21,440 --> 00:05:25,300
So, if it's yours, I'm sorry.

55
00:05:25,300 --> 00:05:27,400
And thank you for letting me borrow it.

56
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:29,640
And you can have it back after service.

57
00:05:29,640 --> 00:05:29,980
Okay.

58
00:05:29,980 --> 00:05:32,020
It's a nice Bible.

59
00:05:32,020 --> 00:05:33,940
If you don't come get it, I might keep it.

60
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:40,980
So, if you would, I'd love for you, once you get there, to stand, and we will read this passage of Scripture together.

61
00:05:40,980 --> 00:05:48,080
Mark 14, starting in verse 1.

62
00:05:48,080 --> 00:06:00,560
And after two days, it was the Passover and the Feast of the Unleavened Bread, and the chief priests and the scribes sought how they might take him, this being Jesus, by trickery and put Jesus to death.

63
00:06:00,560 --> 00:06:05,420
But they said, not during the feast, lest there be an uproar of the people.

64
00:06:05,420 --> 00:06:16,480
And being in Bethany at the house of Simon the leopard, as Jesus sat at the table, a woman came, having an alabaster flask of very costly oil, of spikenard.

65
00:06:16,980 --> 00:06:22,960
Then she broke, she broke the flask, and she poured it on his head.

66
00:06:22,960 --> 00:06:29,460
There were some there who were indignant among themselves and said, why was this fragrant oil wasted?

67
00:06:29,460 --> 00:06:30,780
Wasted.

68
00:06:30,780 --> 00:06:37,680
For it might have been sold for more than 300 denarii or close to a year's salary and given to the poor.

69
00:06:37,900 --> 00:06:47,380
But Jesus said, let her alone.

70
00:06:47,380 --> 00:06:49,420
Why do you trouble her?

71
00:06:49,420 --> 00:06:51,980
She has done a good work for me.

72
00:06:51,980 --> 00:06:56,860
For you have the poor with you always, and wherever you wish, you may do good to them.

73
00:06:56,860 --> 00:06:59,520
But me, you do not always have.

74
00:06:59,520 --> 00:07:01,820
She has done what she could.

75
00:07:02,600 --> 00:07:06,320
She has come beforehand to anoint my body for burial.

76
00:07:06,320 --> 00:07:10,340
Assuredly, I say to you, wherever the gospel is preached.

77
00:07:10,340 --> 00:07:12,460
Listen to this.

78
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:24,060
Assuredly, I say to you, wherever the gospel is preached in the whole world, what this woman has done will also be told as a memorial to her.

79
00:07:25,180 --> 00:07:29,960
Then Judas Iscariot, one of the twelve, went to the chief priest to betray him to them.

80
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:32,660
And when they heard it, they were glad.

81
00:07:32,660 --> 00:07:36,000
And they promised to give him money.

82
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:40,160
So he sought how he might conveniently betray him.

83
00:07:40,160 --> 00:07:42,020
This is the word of the Lord.

84
00:07:42,020 --> 00:07:44,440
We're getting there.

85
00:07:44,440 --> 00:07:45,360
All right, you can be seated.

86
00:07:50,120 --> 00:07:51,300
I don't know about you.

87
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:55,180
Some of you are probably familiar with this story on some level or another.

88
00:07:55,180 --> 00:07:57,700
I grew up in church, went to a Bible college.

89
00:07:57,700 --> 00:07:59,200
So I'm familiar with the story.

90
00:07:59,200 --> 00:08:00,200
It's told here in Mark.

91
00:08:00,200 --> 00:08:01,600
It's also told in John.

92
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,520
And the woman in Mark here is an unnamed woman.

93
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:08,140
But John names her as Mary, the sister of Lazarus.

94
00:08:08,140 --> 00:08:10,540
So if you don't mind, I'm just going to call her Mary the rest of the morning.

95
00:08:10,540 --> 00:08:15,060
Just so that it's convenient and easy for us to grasp.

96
00:08:15,720 --> 00:08:21,380
But no matter how familiar you might be with the story, something strikes me as odd.

97
00:08:21,380 --> 00:08:28,260
Well, there's a lot of things that strike me as odd in the passage because I don't live in first century, you know.

98
00:08:28,260 --> 00:08:32,020
So breaking oil, pouring it on a guy's head, all of that's weird.

99
00:08:32,020 --> 00:08:35,140
But here's the thing that most strikes me as odd.

100
00:08:35,140 --> 00:08:35,980
Verse 9.

101
00:08:35,980 --> 00:08:42,900
Assuredly, I say to you, wherever, this is Jesus, wherever the gospel is preached,

102
00:08:43,900 --> 00:08:52,840
in the whole world, for all time, what this woman has done will be told as a memorial to her?

103
00:08:52,840 --> 00:08:56,500
Is that odd to anybody else or just to me?

104
00:08:56,500 --> 00:08:59,400
Alright, pretty weird, right?

105
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:03,620
Depending on your familiarity with this story and your personality,

106
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:11,360
you might view this moment, this woman breaking this expensive perfume bottle and dumping it all over Jesus.

107
00:09:11,360 --> 00:09:16,800
And John says that she then moves from his head to his feet and uses her hair to wipe his feet.

108
00:09:16,800 --> 00:09:19,880
Depending on your familiarity, your personality,

109
00:09:19,880 --> 00:09:25,420
you either view this as like a beautiful gesture from a loving disciple,

110
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:31,720
or an insane move by an attention-seeking crazy person, right?

111
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:39,640
Shattering a 12-ounce jar of strong perfume and dumping it on an unsuspecting rabbi at a dinner party.

112
00:09:39,640 --> 00:09:42,540
That's kind of a party foul, right?

113
00:09:42,540 --> 00:09:45,960
Anybody else like, hey, you just ruined everyone's night.

114
00:09:47,300 --> 00:09:50,140
So, wherever you fall on the spectrum of responses,

115
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:56,460
I think we can all agree that it seems weird that Jesus editorializes this moment in this way.

116
00:09:56,460 --> 00:10:01,420
He pauses everything to focus on this woman's action,

117
00:10:01,420 --> 00:10:05,680
and he says, wherever the gospel is preached for all time,

118
00:10:05,800 --> 00:10:07,280
all across the world,

119
00:10:07,280 --> 00:10:11,540
this woman's story will be told as a memorial to her.

120
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:15,700
I don't know about you,

121
00:10:15,700 --> 00:10:19,260
but of all the stories and all the Gospels,

122
00:10:19,260 --> 00:10:24,780
this story does not make my top 10 list of the stories that should be told all over the world,

123
00:10:24,780 --> 00:10:27,360
everywhere, wherever the gospel is preached.

124
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:28,460
Am I the only one?

125
00:10:28,460 --> 00:10:30,800
Okay, I'm the only one.

126
00:10:30,800 --> 00:10:31,000
Cool.

127
00:10:31,000 --> 00:10:33,140
I mean, casting out demons, healing blind,

128
00:10:33,140 --> 00:10:34,960
making the lame walk,

129
00:10:34,960 --> 00:10:36,420
resurrecting people from the dead.

130
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:40,980
Why is Jesus so set on this story lasting for thousands of years?

131
00:10:40,980 --> 00:10:42,320
What might,

132
00:10:42,320 --> 00:10:44,060
my notes say,

133
00:10:44,060 --> 00:10:45,100
what might we be missing?

134
00:10:45,100 --> 00:10:47,120
But it sounds like I'm the only one with a problem here.

135
00:10:47,120 --> 00:10:47,400
So,

136
00:10:47,400 --> 00:10:52,460
what might I be missing that makes this story so profound for all people,

137
00:10:52,460 --> 00:10:53,600
for all time?

138
00:10:53,600 --> 00:10:58,460
What makes this story so integral in telling the story of the gospel?

139
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:02,740
This is our overarching task for the morning.

140
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:06,480
What is Jesus emphasizing about this woman's actions?

141
00:11:06,480 --> 00:11:10,880
And what impact might it have on our lives and our telling of the gospel?

142
00:11:10,880 --> 00:11:13,500
Sound good?

143
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,060
All right.

144
00:11:15,060 --> 00:11:16,920
So, in order to get there,

145
00:11:16,920 --> 00:11:18,260
we need to examine,

146
00:11:18,260 --> 00:11:19,980
what do we do in Bible study?

147
00:11:19,980 --> 00:11:22,720
Context, right?

148
00:11:22,720 --> 00:11:27,760
We need to examine the context to get a foundation for why her actions are so important.

149
00:11:27,760 --> 00:11:33,620
Because the reality is her actions are in stark contrast to all the other characters in the passage.

150
00:11:33,620 --> 00:11:35,800
So, verse 1.

151
00:11:35,800 --> 00:11:37,440
This is,

152
00:11:37,440 --> 00:11:40,460
just for timeline's sake,

153
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:42,560
after Lazarus was raised from the dead,

154
00:11:42,560 --> 00:11:44,640
that's why we read that passage in worship,

155
00:11:44,640 --> 00:11:47,960
about a week before Jesus is going to be crucified,

156
00:11:47,960 --> 00:11:49,480
a couple days before Jesus is going to be crucified.

157
00:11:49,680 --> 00:11:51,420
So, after two days,

158
00:11:51,420 --> 00:11:52,860
it was the Passover,

159
00:11:52,860 --> 00:11:55,180
at the Feast of the Unleavened Bread,

160
00:11:55,180 --> 00:11:58,040
and the chief priests and the scribes,

161
00:11:58,040 --> 00:11:59,640
two different groups,

162
00:11:59,640 --> 00:12:00,980
two different categories of people,

163
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:02,160
they came together

164
00:12:02,160 --> 00:12:05,480
to see how they might take Jesus

165
00:12:05,480 --> 00:12:07,400
by trickery

166
00:12:07,400 --> 00:12:08,560
and put him

167
00:12:08,560 --> 00:12:09,660
to death.

168
00:12:09,660 --> 00:12:11,700
So,

169
00:12:11,700 --> 00:12:14,140
we got two groups of characters here

170
00:12:14,140 --> 00:12:16,580
that are plotting against Jesus.

171
00:12:16,580 --> 00:12:18,240
What is their motivation?

172
00:12:18,240 --> 00:12:21,460
Why might they be plotting against Jesus?

173
00:12:21,460 --> 00:12:22,440
Two groups,

174
00:12:22,440 --> 00:12:23,180
chief priests

175
00:12:23,180 --> 00:12:24,180
and the scribes.

176
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:25,200
Again,

177
00:12:25,200 --> 00:12:26,900
this is the final week of Christ's life on earth.

178
00:12:26,900 --> 00:12:30,240
The tension around Christ's life and his teachings is increasing.

179
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:32,240
The temperature is rising in the room.

180
00:12:32,240 --> 00:12:34,600
Everyone knows something has to happen.

181
00:12:35,420 --> 00:12:37,400
He's raised a person from the dead.

182
00:12:37,400 --> 00:12:40,660
That tends to raise the emotional energy in the room, right?

183
00:12:40,660 --> 00:12:41,880
We know this.

184
00:12:41,880 --> 00:12:44,420
Those of us that believe in a resurrected Savior

185
00:12:44,420 --> 00:12:46,420
have conversations with atheist friends

186
00:12:46,420 --> 00:12:48,020
or people that reject Jesus

187
00:12:48,020 --> 00:12:48,960
and we claim,

188
00:12:48,960 --> 00:12:49,380
hey,

189
00:12:49,380 --> 00:12:50,820
he rose from the dead.

190
00:12:50,820 --> 00:12:51,560
What happens?

191
00:12:51,560 --> 00:12:53,340
Emotions rise, right?

192
00:12:53,340 --> 00:12:54,840
Because somebody thinks we're nuts

193
00:12:54,840 --> 00:12:55,500
and we're like,

194
00:12:55,500 --> 00:12:56,180
nope,

195
00:12:56,180 --> 00:12:56,920
I've seen it.

196
00:12:56,920 --> 00:12:58,040
I've experienced it.

197
00:12:58,040 --> 00:12:58,720
It's happened.

198
00:12:58,720 --> 00:13:00,220
And everyone else is like,

199
00:13:00,220 --> 00:13:00,860
you're nuts.

200
00:13:00,860 --> 00:13:03,440
Emotional temperature rises.

201
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:05,600
And so that's what's going on here.

202
00:13:05,600 --> 00:13:06,660
And this all comes to a head

203
00:13:06,660 --> 00:13:09,840
with Lazarus being raised from the dead.

204
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:10,100
Now,

205
00:13:10,100 --> 00:13:13,860
we don't have time for a full history lesson

206
00:13:13,860 --> 00:13:16,100
around Jewish culture,

207
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:18,440
how the temple and the priests and the Pharisees

208
00:13:18,440 --> 00:13:20,300
and how they all work together this morning.

209
00:13:20,300 --> 00:13:22,580
And even if we had time,

210
00:13:22,580 --> 00:13:25,220
guess who's probably not the most qualified person

211
00:13:25,220 --> 00:13:26,000
to give you that lesson?

212
00:13:26,000 --> 00:13:27,920
Me.

213
00:13:27,920 --> 00:13:28,380
Okay.

214
00:13:29,140 --> 00:13:29,580
All right.

215
00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:33,320
But I'll give you some overarching realities here.

216
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:34,180
Chief priests,

217
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:36,720
they are predominantly Sadducees

218
00:13:36,720 --> 00:13:38,860
and the Sadducees were in charge

219
00:13:38,860 --> 00:13:41,200
of running the temple in Jerusalem.

220
00:13:41,200 --> 00:13:42,160
Okay.

221
00:13:42,160 --> 00:13:46,520
So they like oversaw the temple in Jerusalem.

222
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:48,800
Their ancestors were an integral part

223
00:13:48,800 --> 00:13:50,940
in striking a political deal

224
00:13:50,940 --> 00:13:51,920
with the Romans

225
00:13:51,920 --> 00:13:53,060
that allowed the Jews

226
00:13:53,060 --> 00:13:55,460
to continue to worship God in the temple.

227
00:13:55,460 --> 00:13:57,580
The Jews were the only group in the Roman world

228
00:13:57,580 --> 00:13:59,840
that were allowed to just worship their God

229
00:13:59,840 --> 00:14:01,920
and ignore the Roman gods, right?

230
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:03,240
And the chief priests,

231
00:14:03,240 --> 00:14:04,240
their family members,

232
00:14:04,240 --> 00:14:05,680
their ancestors were the ones

233
00:14:05,680 --> 00:14:08,160
that made this alliance with Rome

234
00:14:08,160 --> 00:14:10,160
so that they could continue

235
00:14:10,160 --> 00:14:11,800
worshiping God in the temple.

236
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:13,100
Does that sound like a pretty good thing?

237
00:14:13,100 --> 00:14:15,540
Okay.

238
00:14:15,540 --> 00:14:17,220
It does sound like a pretty good thing,

239
00:14:17,220 --> 00:14:18,080
but it wasn't

240
00:14:18,080 --> 00:14:19,840
because the Sadducees,

241
00:14:19,840 --> 00:14:21,100
they really weren't concerned

242
00:14:21,100 --> 00:14:22,400
about worshiping God properly

243
00:14:22,400 --> 00:14:24,340
as much as they were concerned

244
00:14:24,340 --> 00:14:25,420
about the wealth,

245
00:14:25,420 --> 00:14:27,080
cultural influence,

246
00:14:27,080 --> 00:14:27,900
and power

247
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:29,000
that came with them

248
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:30,400
running the temple,

249
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:33,240
having this political alliance with Rome.

250
00:14:33,240 --> 00:14:34,960
They were quite literally

251
00:14:34,960 --> 00:14:36,680
married to Rome

252
00:14:36,680 --> 00:14:38,140
and they were using

253
00:14:38,140 --> 00:14:39,860
